  "notes": "The best-documented construction recipe in this section, and it is documented as a *contradiction* the model has to hold: the entire album was programmed on an SP-1200 with the explicit goal of \"merging East Coast drums with West Coast sounds\". So the kit is boom-bap tight — crisp syncopated kick at 3–5 with the secondary anchor on `3&`, ghost notes at `prob` 0.34 on the 16ths before the backbeat, patterns written to emulate a live drummer (`quantizeStrength` 0.8, `velocityVar` 0.2) — while everything melodic keeps West Coast sustain. ⛔ `chords.impliedByRiff` is set because the research explains exactly why the harmony is thin: it comes from sampled jazz instrumentation and sampled Moog rather than from a keyboard part, so voicings are 1–2 notes and \"progressions reduce to `i–VII` or a static `i`\". Both of those are authored at weight 4, the `extensions` are 0.86 triad — the least coloured chord block in this batch — and the variety comes from `chordDurationBeats` 2–12 rather than from families nobody plays. ⚠ **`i–iv` at weight 2 is the one INFERRED family**; the research names only `i–VII` and static `i`. ⚠ The hook sounds are samples triggered from SP-1200 pads, not MIDI-played synths, which is why the melody is staccato at 2–5 notes a bar with `motifPitchCount` 2–4 and `timbreHint` `horn_stab`; the bass is the same machine's pitch-shifted samples, short and punchy with no long sub tails, so `mirror_kick` with `glideProb` 0. ⚠ `transitions.switchUpProb` is 0 because the research says it plainly: no beat switches, and songs were delivered musically complete before vocals. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 45 to 42 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 43 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 3.",
